# There is nothing here, and the reason is worth keeping.
#
# docs/DECISIONS.md was one append-only file carrying every settled decision,
# and it was set `merge=union` so that concurrent appends were not a conflict.
# The reasoning was sound as far as it went:
#
#   The decision record is append-only, so concurrent appends are not a
#   conflict. Every pull request that settles something adds an entry at the
#   end of this file. Without a merge driver, the first one to merge puts every
#   other open branch into conflict on it -- three at the time this was added
#   (#4, #5, #7), none of which disagreed about anything. `union` keeps both
#   sides instead of asking, which is the right answer for a log that only ever
#   grows.
#
# It named its own failure mode and its own remedy, and both arrived:
#
#   When it stops being right: `union` turns an in-place edit into silent
#   duplication rather than a conflict. [...] If entries ever do start being
#   edited in place, the answer is one file per decision
#   (docs/decisions/USOSS-N.md), where separate files cannot conflict at all --
#   a migration, not a merge attribute.
#
# What it did not anticipate is that the attribute never worked where it
# mattered. A merge driver is a property of the local git configuration.
# GitHub's mergeability computation does not honour .gitattributes merge
# drivers, so `git merge-tree --write-tree origin/main origin/<branch>` exited 0
# with no conflicts for #5, #19, #26 and #27 while GitHub reported all four
# CONFLICTING -- over docs/DECISIONS.md, the only file main had touched since
# those branches diverged. And a CONFLICTING pull request gets no CI at all,
# because `pull_request` workflows need a refs/pull/N/merge that cannot be
# built. Thirteen open pull requests, seven-plus of them conflicted per merge,
# all of them displaying a stale green tick.
#
# So the migration happened, for a different reason than the one predicted. The
# record is now one file per decision under docs/decisions/, where separate
# files cannot conflict -- locally or on GitHub. See that directory's README,
# internal/decisions for the invariants, and the decision entry recording this.
